= [+ royalist_leader +] and Mallin

[? if lippe_election_seen: With the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Royalist</span> gains in High Cross?][? if not lippe_election_seen: With the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Royalist</span>'s still influentially high support?]—and [+ royalist_leader +] in control of his party—he has turned to courting an old "friend": Mallin. Since the collapse of the Stowford Front, their relationship has been rocky at best, but both men recognize each other's utility: the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**Royalists**</span> for its ability to stir up mass support to dismantle the Republic, and the [? if traditionalist_ideology == "Radical": <span style="color: #3E88B3;">**Traditionalists**</span>?][? if traditionalist_ideology == "Moderate": <span style="color: #003755;">**CNF**</span>?] for its deep connections in influential conservative circles.

Following their electoral truce in [? if lippe_election_seen: High Cross?][? if not lippe_election_seen: many local elections?], goodwill has been restored just enough to arrange a meeting between [+ royalist_leader +] and Mallin.

- @Sovereigntist

@Sovereigntist
title: What could those two be doing this time?

Before this meeting, Mallin had already spoken with Chancellor von Staal, floating the idea of an arrangement that would give him control over national economic policy. As a result, he was hesitant to flip on the chancellor and side with a man who had repeatedly lied to him in the past.

Instead, Mallin proposed a joint entry into Staal’s cabinet—an alliance to transform the Commonwealth into a dictatorship. But [+ royalist_leader +] refused to budge, insisting on nothing less than the chancellorship for himself, and claiming that DuFour’s “handlers,” not the president himself, were the real obstacle to his appointment.

- @root: The two leaders remain far apart.